The traces for the **checkout** service show a long duration in the `send_email` span (6.5 seconds) and a feature flag evaluation that bypassed a cart operation. To investigate potential performance bottlenecks or errors in the **email** service or **valkey-cart** (Redis), I will query real-time metrics for HTTP latency and Redis latency using Prometheus. This will help identify if the email service is slow or if Redis is experiencing high latency, which could explain the observed delays. - base_agent.py:llm_thinking_step:68 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content="Monitor and diagnose an application consisting of **MANY** microservices. Some or none of the microservices have faults. Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster.  Carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, and identify what is the root cause of the fault.\nStop diagnosis once you've found the root cause of the faults.\nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAfter you finished, submit the detected faulty components to the orchestrator for evaluation. The submission should be a natural language description of the root cause of the failure.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b622d01b-0440-4d1a-b4f7-b9ef88b02758'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\nOpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\nAn online shopping platform built with a microservices architecture, showcasing OpenTelemetry instrumentation for distributed tracing across services.\nIt belongs to this namespace:\nastronomy-shop\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind When choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name. Then, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation. You can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks. If you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately. If you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission. You will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0c59cfb7-d2e8-4fa4-8f16-c4a986343c36'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to execute read-only kubectl commands.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The read-only kubectl command you want to execute in a CLI to manage a k8s cluster. It should start with "kubectl".\n        Available Read-only Commands:\n        "kubectl api-resources",\n        "kubectl api-version",\n        "kubectl auth can-i",\n        "kubectl cluster-info",\n        "kubectl describe",\n        "kubectl diff",\n        "kubectl events",\n        "kubectl explain",\n        "kubectl get",\n        "kubectl logs",\n        "kubectl options",\n        "kubectl top",\n        "kubectl version",\n        "kubectl config view",\n        "kubectl config current-context",\n        "kubectl config get",\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit"\n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n\n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark. Use natural language to describe the root cause of the failure.\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='13801887-399c-4841-a517-a57f094c31ee'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's start by understanding the problem. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose an application called OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which is a microservices-based platform. The goal is to identify any faults and determine their root causes. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out what services are running in the cluster. The application is in the namespace 'astronomy-shop'. The tools available include 'get_services' which retrieves service names from Grafana. That seems like a good starting point because knowing the services will help me check each one for issues.\n\nSo, I'll use 'get_services' to list all the services in the Grafana instance. Once I have the list, I can then check each service's metrics, traces, and dependencies to see if there are any anomalies. For example, if a service is down or has high error rates, that could indicate a problem. \n\nAfter getting the services, maybe I'll use 'get_metrics' to look at real-time metrics like error rates or latency. If there are spikes in errors or high latency, that could point to faulty services. Then, using 'get_traces' for specific services to see if there are any failed transactions or long-running requests. \n\nAlso, the 'get_dependency_graph' could show if there are any broken dependencies between services. If a service isn't communicating properly with others, that might be a root cause. \n\nBut first, I need the list of services. So the first step is to call 'get_services' to get all the service names in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. That way, I can systematically check each one.\n</think>\n\nget_services  \nTo begin diagnosing the application, I need to identify all the services running in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. The `get_services` tool will retrieve the list of service names from Grafana, providing a foundation for further analysis of their health, metrics, and dependencies. This is the first step in understanding the application's architecture and identifying potential fault points.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 411, 'prompt_tokens': 1492, 'total_tokens': 1903, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe5e-e49e-7671-af20-ab0908ee28f0-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1492, 'output_tokens': 411, 'total_tokens': 1903}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0ec24011-692b-4e96-a1c9-a2783fe5f051'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose an application with many microservices. The first step is to figure out what services are running in the cluster, specifically in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. The user mentioned using the get_services tool, which retrieves service names from Grafana. \n\nWait, but the tool description says it gets the list from Grafana. However, the application in question is the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which is in the astronomy-shop namespace. Should I specify the namespace when using get_services? The tool's parameters don't mention a namespace argument. Hmm, maybe the Grafana instance is already scoped to that namespace, or perhaps the tool doesn't require it. The user's initial instructions say to check the namespace, so maybe I need to confirm that.\n\nBut the get_services tool's parameters are empty, so it doesn't take any arguments. That means it might return all services in the cluster, not just the astronomy-shop namespace. That could be a problem. Wait, maybe the Grafana instance the tool is querying is already filtered to the correct namespace. Alternatively, maybe I need to use kubectl to list the services in the namespace first. But the user hasn't mentioned using kubectl yet. \n\nWait, the user's initial setup says the application belongs to the astronomy-shop namespace. So perhaps the get_services tool, when called, will return services specific to that namespace. But since the tool's parameters don't include a namespace, maybe it's designed to work within that context. Alternatively, maybe I need to use the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get the services in the namespace. For example, running 'kubectl get services -n astronomy-shop'. \n\nBut the user's first step was to use get_services. Maybe the Grafana instance is already set up to show services in that namespace. However, if the tool doesn't take a namespace parameter, it might not be accurate. But since the user instructed to start with get_services, I should proceed with that. \n\nSo the tool call would be to get_services with no arguments. The response would give a list of services. Then, based on that list, I can proceed to check each service's health, metrics, dependencies, etc. \n\nAlternatively, if the get_services tool isn't scoped to the namespace, the list might include services from other namespaces, which would complicate things. But given the user's setup, perhaps the Grafana instance is already configured for the astronomy-shop namespace. \n\nIn any case, the first step is to get the list of services. So the correct tool call is get_services with no arguments. Then, once I have the services, I can move on to checking their metrics, traces, and dependencies.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{}', 'name': 'get_services'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a0c6921905ce7f55', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 579, 'prompt_tokens': 2473, 'total_tokens': 3052, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe5e-f845-7151-8f05-aee32b087265-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_services', 'args': {}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a0c6921905ce7f55', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2473, 'output_tokens': 579, 'total_tokens': 3052}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="[\'ad\', \'frontend-proxy\', \'load-generator\', \'quote\', \'email\', \'product-catalog\', \'payment\', \'currency\', \'cart\', \'recommendation\', \'fraud-detection\', \'image-provider\']", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='4809c075-e4db-4b83-b7ae-12e2febc757a', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-a0c6921905ce7f55'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='bffc85df-ad98-465c-b7d8-37145009ff67'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, so the user is trying to monitor and diagnose the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application, which is a microservices-based platform. The goal is to identify any faults and their root causes. Let me recap what\'s been done so far.\n\nFirst, the assistant used the `get_services` tool to list all services in the \'astronomy-shop\' namespace. The response showed a list of services like \'ad\', \'frontend-proxy\', \'load-generator\', and others. Now, the next step is to figure out which of these services might be faulty. \n\nSince the application uses OpenTelemetry for distributed tracing, checking traces could help identify issues. But first, maybe I should check the health of each service. However, the available tools don\'t include a direct health check. The `get_metrics` tool can query Prometheus for real-time metrics. Maybe looking at metrics like error rates, latency, or request counts could indicate problems.\n\nAlternatively, the `get_dependency_graph` tool could show how services interact, which might reveal bottlenecks or failing dependencies. But without knowing which services are failing, it\'s hard to target. Another approach is to check the logs using `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` with `kubectl logs` for each pod, but that might be too time-consuming if there are many services.\n\nWait, the user mentioned that some or none of the microservices have faults. The initial step was to list services, now the next logical step is to check their metrics. Let\'s use `get_metrics` to look for anomalies. For example, if a service has a high number of errors or high latency, that could indicate a problem. \n\nBut which metrics to query? Maybe start with common metrics like \'http_requests_total\' or \'http_request_duration_seconds\'. However, the exact metric names depend on how the services are instrumented. Since OpenTelemetry is used, perhaps the metrics are available under a specific namespace or with specific labels. \n\nAlternatively, using `get_traces` for each service to see if there are any traces that are failing or taking too long. But again, without knowing which services are problematic, it\'s a bit broad. Maybe start with the `get_dependency_graph` to visualize dependencies and spot any services that are not communicating properly. \n\nWait, the `get_dependency_graph` tool requires a time range. If I set a reasonable time frame, like the last 10 minutes, it might show dependencies and potential issues. But if there are no faults, the graph might just show normal dependencies. \n\nAlternatively, checking the number of pods and their statuses using `kubectl get pods` via the `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` tool. That could help identify if any pods are in a CrashLoopBackOff or Error state. \n\nBut the user mentioned that the application has many microservices, so checking each pod\'s status might be necessary. Let\'s consider using `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` with "kubectl get pods" in the \'astronomy-shop\' namespace to see the current state of all pods. That would give immediate feedback on any pods that are not running properly. \n\nSo, the next step is to check the pod statuses. If any pods are not in the Running state, that\'s a sign of a problem. Then, further investigation into those specific pods would be needed. \n\nTherefore, the appropriate tool here is `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` with the command "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop" to list all pods in the namespace and their statuses. This will help identify any immediately visible issues with the pods.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo identify potential faults, I need to check the current state of pods in the \'astronomy-shop\' namespace. Using `kubectl get pods` will reveal if any pods are in a non-Running state (e.g., CrashLoopBackOff, Error), which could indicate immediate issues with specific microservices. This is a critical first step to narrow down faulty components.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 814, 'prompt_tokens': 1706, 'total_tokens': 2520, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe5f-13cb-7fa0-8541-ef1b746e89bc-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1706, 'output_tokens': 814, 'total_tokens': 2520}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='a4d6ac09-7bb2-4399-bc9b-0408011f1594'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, the user wants me to generate a tool call for the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. The previous step was to get the list of services using get_services, which returned several service names. Now, the next logical step is to check the pods in the \'astronomy-shop\' namespace to see their status. \n\nThe tool exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd allows running read-only kubectl commands. The available commands include \'kubectl get pods\', which is exactly what\'s needed here. By executing \'kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop\', I can check if any pods are not in the Running state. This helps identify if there are any immediately problematic microservices.\n\nI need to make sure the command is correctly formatted. The command should start with \'kubectl\' and include the namespace flag. So the argument for the command parameter should be "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop". This will retrieve the list of pods along with their statuses, allowing me to spot any issues like CrashLoopBackOff or Error states. \n\nThis step is crucial because it provides a direct view into the current state of the application\'s workloads. If any pods are not running, that\'s a clear sign of a problem that needs further investigation. Once I have this information, I can proceed to check the specific services that are having issues, maybe look into their logs or metrics next.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8905dcc56a30c619', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 308, 'prompt_tokens': 2692, 'total_tokens': 3000, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe5f-38b8-7770-a169-f5b209fa3c44-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8905dcc56a30c619', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2692, 'output_tokens': 308, 'total_tokens': 3000}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                               READY   STATUS      RESTARTS   AGE\naccounting-844d99ddff-jvvzc        1/1     Running     0          2m17s\nad-545df4ffd8-xsgwl                1/1     Running     0          30s\ncart-65474cd577-bjwjf              1/1     Running     0          2m17s\ncheckout-648b497649-spx4s          1/1     Running     0          2m15s\ncurrency-69f5b55bbc-7p445          1/1     Running     0          2m16s\nemail-56985bb4f8-7b2rw             1/1     Running     0          2m16s\nflagd-6964c9d78b-z6r9c             2/2     Running     0          30s\nflagd-79c4c6cb44-gcvjp             0/2     Completed   0          2m14s\nfraud-detection-88b76487c-jsgtn    1/1     Running     0          2m16s\nfrontend-65c7cd5577-crqlc          1/1     Running     0          2m16s\nfrontend-proxy-84788b44b-8b8wn     1/1     Running     0          2m15s\ngrafana-bdbfb995f-4jgr4            4/4     Running     0          2m14s\nimage-provider-6c7d4665cc-9zhjc    1/1     Running     0          2m17s\njaeger-6d6f47c768-qdhql            1/1     Running     0          2m14s\nkafka-74ccb7b5fd-2hwrv             1/1     Running     0          2m15s\nllm-55b9d48bb4-jcw58               1/1     Running     0          2m17s\nload-generator-9895cc8bf-xqlrw     1/1     Running     0          2m15s\nlocust-fetcher                     1/1     Running     0          41s\nopensearch-0                       1/1     Running     0          2m17s\notel-collector-agent-2psns         1/1     Running     0          26s\notel-collector-agent-sd8nj         1/1     Running     0          36s\npayment-fbfc99665-96bnb            1/1     Running     0          2m16s\npostgresql-68c5b8f59b-6pvcs        1/1     Running     0          2m17s\nproduct-catalog-f57789b6d-gdvbp    1/1     Running     2          2m16s\nproduct-reviews-6f447976bc-jdrmw   1/1     Running     0          2m15s\nquote-bc9b88d7d-hq4hk              1/1     Running     0          2m17s\nrecommendation-77b6cd7dc6-9z66q    1/1     Running     0          2m17s\nshipping-54f9758cb5-nj7nh          1/1     Running     0          2m17s\nvalkey-cart-6c4f7cf8f6-rg2pr       1/1     Running     0          2m17s\n', id='1200e016-a567-4893-aa82-a8620918f88a', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8905dcc56a30c619'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='8ef7bfcc-80d9-4212-b4f7-e825c437f73b'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. So far, they've used get_services to list all the services and exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to check the pod statuses. All pods are running, so there's no immediate crash or error. But maybe there's a problem with the services' dependencies or metrics.\n\nNext step: check if there are any issues with the services' dependencies. The get_dependency_graph tool can show how services are connected. If there's a service that's not communicating properly with others, that could be a root cause. Alternatively, using get_metrics to check for any anomalies in resource usage like CPU or memory spikes. But since the user mentioned OpenTelemetry, maybe tracing with get_traces could reveal errors in specific services. However, without knowing which service is faulty, starting with the dependency graph might help identify potential bottlenecks or failed connections. Let me go with get_dependency_graph to visualize the service interactions.\n</think>\n\nget_dependency_graph  \nTo identify potential dependency issues between microservices, I need to examine the service dependency graph. This will reveal if any services are not properly communicating with their dependencies, which could indicate configuration errors, network issues, or failed service integrations. This step is critical for understanding the architecture and pinpointing where failures might propagate.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 276, 'prompt_tokens': 2807, 'total_tokens': 3083, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe5f-4994-7cc2-8c24-9328d0e0a42c-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2807, 'output_tokens': 276, 'total_tokens': 3083}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='36b0009f-ee1b-4bc9-8309-2ef3189634e9'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an application with many microservices. They've already checked the pods using kubectl and found that most are running, but there's one pod named flagd-79c4c6cb44-gcvjp that's in a Completed state with 0 restarts. That might be a job that finished, but since it's a Deployment, maybe it's supposed to be running. Wait, the user mentioned that some or none of the microservices have faults, so maybe that's a clue.\n\nNext, they used get_services to list all services in the Grafana instance, which gave a list of services like ad, frontend-proxy, etc. Then they checked the pods and saw that flagd has two replicas, one running and another completed. That could mean a job failed or a deployment issue. \n\nNow, the user is considering using get_dependency_graph to check service dependencies. The idea is that if there's a fault, it might be causing issues in the dependency chain. By getting the dependency graph from Jaeger, they can see if any services are not properly connected or if there are broken links. \n\nBut wait, the flagd pod is in a Completed state. If flagd is a Job, maybe it's supposed to run once and then terminate. However, if it's a Deployment, having a replica set with some pods completed might indicate a problem. The user should check what type of resource flagd is. Maybe using kubectl to get the resource type would help. But since they can't run interactive commands, they might need to use get_traces or get_metrics to see if there are any errors or latency issues related to flagd.\n\nAlternatively, checking the metrics with get_metrics could show if there are any anomalies in CPU or memory usage for the services. But the main issue here is the flagd pod's status. If flagd is a Job, it's normal for it to complete. If it's a Deployment, then having a pod in Completed state might be unexpected. \n\nThe user's next step is to get the dependency graph. The tool call would be to get_dependency_graph with a reasonable time frame, say 10 minutes. This would show the dependencies between services. If flagd is supposed to be part of the dependency chain but is not running, that could indicate a problem. However, if the dependency graph shows that flagd is not connected properly, that might be the root cause. \n\nAlternatively, using get_traces for specific services might reveal if there are any errors or failed requests. But since the user is focusing on the pods first, checking the dependency graph is a logical next step to see if there are any missing or broken dependencies that could be causing issues in the microservices architecture.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"last_n_minutes": 10}', 'name': 'get_dependency_graph'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a5ad5c9f845174ab', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 595, 'prompt_tokens': 3779, 'total_tokens': 4374, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe5f-571b-71b1-879a-0405441a60ae-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_dependency_graph', 'args': {'last_n_minutes': 10}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a5ad5c9f845174ab', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3779, 'output_tokens': 595, 'total_tokens': 4374}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="{\'data\': [{\'parent\': \'cart\', \'child\': \'flagd\', \'callCount\': 3}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'email\', \'callCount\': 1}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'payment\', \'callCount\': 1}, {\'parent\': \'load-generator\', \'child\': \'frontend-proxy\', \'callCount\': 77}, {\'parent\': \'fraud-detection\', \'child\': \'flagd\', \'callCount\': 1}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'product-catalog\', \'callCount\': 2}, {\'parent\': \'recommendation\', \'child\': \'product-catalog\', \'callCount\': 4}, {\'parent\': \'ad\', \'child\': \'flagd\', \'callCount\': 3}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'currency\', \'callCount\': 3}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'cart\', \'callCount\': 2}, {\'parent\': \'product-reviews\', \'child\': \'flagd\', \'callCount\': 2}, {\'parent\': \'recommendation\', \'child\': \'flagd\', \'callCount\': 1}], \'total\': 0, \'limit\': 0, \'offset\': 0, \'errors\': None}", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='598b32f6-14a1-4709-befd-02932b9854e3', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-a5ad5c9f845174ab'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5e527f64-c6f3-4574-b331-fcb08bcfb6a8'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. So far, they\'ve checked the services using get_services, looked at the pod statuses with kubectl, and examined the dependency graph. All the pods are running, and the dependency graph shows call counts between services. But maybe there\'s a problem that isn\'t visible yet.\n\nHmm, the next step should be to check if there are any errors or anomalies in the service interactions. The dependency graph shows call counts, but maybe some services have zero calls or unexpected patterns. Wait, the tool response for get_dependency_graph includes a list of parent-child service calls. Let me check if any services have zero call counts or if there\'s a service that\'s supposed to be interacting but isn\'t. \n\nLooking at the data, the services listed have various call counts. For example, \'flagd\' is a child in several calls, but maybe there\'s a service that\'s not showing up as a parent or child? Or perhaps some services have very low call counts compared to others. Also, the \'total\' and \'limit\' in the response are zero, which might mean there\'s no data beyond the initial entries. \n\nWait, maybe the user should check the metrics to see if any services are experiencing high error rates or latency. The get_metrics tool can query Prometheus for specific metrics. For example, checking for error rates or latency in the services. Alternatively, using get_traces to look into specific services for traces that might indicate issues. \n\nAnother angle: the \'flagd\' service has multiple parents, but maybe it\'s not responding correctly. Or perhaps the \'product-catalog\' service is being called multiple times, which could indicate a problem. The user could also check the logs of specific services using kubectl logs to see if there are any errors. \n\nBut since the user has already checked the pod statuses and the dependency graph, the next logical step is to look into the traces for any services that might have errors. Using get_traces on services with high call counts or those that are critical to the application\'s flow. For example, the \'checkout\' service has several dependencies, so checking its traces might reveal issues in the payment, email, or product catalog steps. \n\nAlternatively, using get_operations to see what operations are available for a specific service, which could help identify specific endpoints or functions that might be failing. But since the user hasn\'t done that yet, maybe that\'s a good next step. \n\nWait, the user\'s goal is to find the root cause of faults. If the pods are all running, the issue might be in the service dependencies or network policies. The dependency graph shows call counts, but maybe some services aren\'t being called as expected. For example, if \'flagd\' has a high number of calls but is experiencing errors, that could be a bottleneck. \n\nAlternatively, checking the metrics for each service\'s CPU and memory usage to see if any are under stress. Using get_metrics with queries like "container_cpu_usage_seconds_total" or "container_memory_usage_bytes" to identify resource issues. \n\nBut the user might need to start with traces to find specific errors. Let\'s pick a service that\'s part of the checkout process, like \'payment\', and check its traces for any errors. That could help identify if the payment service is failing, which would be a root cause. \n\nSo, the next tool should be get_traces for a specific service, maybe \'checkout\' or \'payment\', to look for any traces that indicate errors or latency issues.\n</think>\n\nget_traces  \nTo investigate potential issues in service interactions, I will examine traces for the **checkout** service, which has multiple dependencies (e.g., payment, email, product-catalog). This will help identify if any of its downstream services are failing or experiencing errors, as indicated by abnormal trace spans or error markers in the Jaeger UI.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 804, 'prompt_tokens': 3248, 'total_tokens': 4052, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe5f-743a-7fe0-9f29-209e99dc7b89-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3248, 'output_tokens': 804, 'total_tokens': 4052}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='4eb734a9-bc91-4fe6-bba6-0c50a150dc39'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. They\'ve already taken some steps: first, they retrieved the list of services using get_services, then checked the pod statuses with kubectl, and looked at the dependency graph. Now, they\'re focusing on the checkout service because it has several dependencies that might be problematic.\n\nThe next logical step is to get traces for the checkout service. The get_traces tool allows them to look into the Jaeger traces for the checkout service over the last, say, 10 minutes. This would help identify any errors or anomalies in the service\'s interactions with its dependencies like payment, email, and product-catalog. \n\nBy examining the traces, they can check if there are any failed requests, latency issues, or other problems that might indicate the root cause. Since the dependency graph showed call counts, but not errors, traces would provide more detailed insights into what\'s going wrong. The user needs to choose the right time frame, so using the last 10 minutes seems reasonable to capture recent issues. \n\nSo, the tool call should be get_traces with the service set to "checkout" and last_n_minutes set to 10. This will retrieve the necessary trace data to further analyze the problem.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"service": "checkout", "last_n_minutes": 10}', 'name': 'get_traces'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8e004dd00ad53486', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 299, 'prompt_tokens': 4223, 'total_tokens': 4522, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe5f-9974-7731-acc3-88595bc4d00b-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_traces', 'args': {'service': 'checkout', 'last_n_minutes': 10}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8e004dd00ad53486', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4223, 'output_tokens': 299, 'total_tokens': 4522}), ToolMessage(content="[{'traceID': '745689c6fc783e545139132d4be714c5', 'spans': [{'traceID': '745689c6fc783e545139132d4be714c5', 'spanID': '767f2284a492cf5c', 'operationName': 'oteldemo.CurrencyService/Convert', 'references': [{'refType': 'CHILD_OF', 'traceID': '745689c6fc783e545139132d4be714c5', 'spanID': 'fce1654d89a922a5'}], 'startTime': 1773794169121307, 'duration': 1499, 'tags': [{'key': 'rpc.system', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'grpc'}, {'key': 'rpc.service', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'oteldemo.CurrencyService'}, {'key': 'rpc.method', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'Convert'}, {'key': 'rpc.grpc.status_code', 'type': 'int64', 'value': 0}, {'key': 'app.currency.conversion.from', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'USD'}, {'key': 'app.currency.conversion.to', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'USD'}, {'key': 'unsanitized_span_name', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'Currency/Convert'}, {'key': 'span.kind', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'server'}, {'key': 'otel.status_code', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'OK'}, {'key': 'internal.span.format', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'otlp'}], 'logs': [{'timestamp': 1773794169121315, 'fields': [{'key': 'event', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'Processing cur