nction': {'arguments': '{"query": "avg_over_time(redis_command_duration_seconds{job=\\"valkey-cart\\"}[5m])"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-96663c91f9439a31', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 410, 'prompt_tokens': 11990, 'total_tokens': 12400, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe60-5221-7f31-8520-f8524465bc79-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_metrics', 'args': {'query': 'avg_over_time(redis_command_duration_seconds{job="valkey-cart"}[5m])'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-96663c91f9439a31', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11990, 'output_tokens': 410, 'total_tokens': 12400}), ToolMessage(content='("{\'resultType\': \'vector\', \'result\': []}", 12)', id='89bc0da2-fd61-4b3e-9ae3-9056052a9415', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-96663c91f9439a31'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='9bf1bbf5-3b58-4bb1-b449-bbb4e5931ca9'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. They\'ve already checked the pods and found them all running, so the problem isn\'t immediately obvious from the pod statuses. The dependency graph showed some calls, but nothing alarming. The traces for the checkout service included a long send_email span and a feature flag that bypassed a cart operation. \n\nFirst, they tried checking the email service\'s HTTP request duration using Prometheus, but the query didn\'t return any data. Then they checked Redis (valkey-cart) latency metrics, which also came back empty. That suggests either the metric names are wrong, or the services aren\'t properly configured to expose those metrics.\n\nSince the email service\'s metrics aren\'t showing up, maybe the job label isn\'t correct. Perhaps the email service is labeled differently in Prometheus. Alternatively, the metrics might not be exposed at all. The same goes for Redis. Maybe the Redis instance isn\'t sending metrics to Prometheus, or the job name is different.\n\nAnother approach could be to check the actual services\' configurations. For example, the email service might be using a different job name, like "email-service" instead of "email". Similarly, the Redis instance might have a different job label. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the metrics aren\'t being collected because the Prometheus server isn\'t scraping the correct endpoints. The user could check the Prometheus configuration to see if the targets for email and Redis are correctly set up. But since the user can\'t directly access Prometheus, they might need to use another tool or check the service\'s own metrics endpoints.\n\nWait, the user has the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool. Maybe they can check the environment variables or configurations of the email and Redis pods to see if they\'re exposing metrics and under what job names. For example, checking the env vars for the email container to see if it\'s using a specific metric endpoint or job label.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the email service isn\'t exposing HTTP metrics at all. If the email service is a custom application, it might not be instrumented with Prometheus metrics. Similarly, Redis might not be exposing the expected metrics unless it\'s configured with the Redis Exporter.\n\nSo, the next step should be to check the actual metrics endpoints of the email service and Redis. Using kubectl to exec into the pods and check if the metrics endpoints are accessible. For example, checking if the email service has a /metrics endpoint and if Redis is running with the Redis Exporter.\n\nBut the user\'s available tools don\'t include exec into the pods directly. Wait, they do have exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. So they can run commands like "kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- curl http://localhost:8080/metrics" to check if the metrics are available. However, the user needs to know the pod names for email and valkey-cart.\n\nFrom the earlier kubectl get pods output, the email pod is named "email-56985bb4f8-7b2rw". So they can use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to check the metrics endpoint of that pod. Similarly for the valkey-cart pod.\n\nSo the next tool call should be to exec into the email pod and check if the metrics are available. If the email service is using a different port or path for metrics, that could explain why the Prometheus query didn\'t return data. Similarly for Redis.\n\nAlternatively, if the metrics are not exposed, the root cause could be that the email service isn\'t instrumented with Prometheus, leading to no metrics being collected. Or the Redis instance isn\'t using the Redis Exporter, so its metrics aren\'t available.\n\nTherefore, the user should use the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool to check the metrics endpoints of the email and Redis pods. This will help determine if the metrics are being exposed and under what names, which can then be used to adjust the Prometheus queries accordingly.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo debug the missing metrics, I will directly check the **email** service\'s pod to see if it exposes Prometheus metrics (e.g., `/metrics` endpoint) and verify the Redis (**valkey-cart**) pod\'s configuration. This will confirm whether metrics are being exposed and identify potential misconfigurations or missing instrumentation. \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl exec -n astronomy-shop -it email-56985bb4f8-7b2rw -- curl http://localhost:8080/metrics"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': 